Given the platform notes in `PLATFORM.md`: WebSockets are dead at the edge (501 on upgrade), and every function gets killed at 10s with no shared memory between instances — so SSE is also out, since holding a connection open past 10s just forces constant reconnects for no benefit. And `rollup.md` says the data only changes once a minute, on a fixed schedule, so there's nothing to push in real time anyway.

That leaves polling, which is actually a good fit here, not a compromise. Replace the click handler in `public/dashboard.js` with a `setInterval` that calls the same `load()`, and skip re-rendering when `computed_at` hasn't moved:

```js
let lastComputedAt = null;

async function load() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (computed_at === lastComputedAt) return;
  lastComputedAt = computed_at;
  render(rows);
  document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
}

window.addEventListener('load', load);
setInterval(load, 20000);
```

The one real fork: fixed interval (e.g. every 20s) vs. aligning polls to just after the minute boundary since that's when the rollup job actually writes. Fixed interval is simpler and tolerant of clients with wrong clocks or the schedule shifting later; alignment cuts request volume but couples the client to `rollup.md`'s schedule. With a 30ms/4KB response, request volume isn't a real cost here, so I'd take the fixed interval and not bother aligning.

